Job Description
The Institute seeks to appoint a Youth@SAIIA Programmes Manager who is committed to youth development and public participation.
For over a decade, Youth Programmes at the South African Institute of International Affairs (Youth@SAIIA) has been working to mobilise and capacitate children and youth (13-25 years) across South Africa to become more active citizens and effectively participate in decision-making processes that affect their lives.
Youth@SAIIA seeks to make the following appointment:
Youth Programmes Manager (YPM/0624)
1-year contract
|
Salary band based on experience: R380k-R480k p.a
Youth@SAIIA is looking to appoint a dynamic person who is committed to youth development and public participation from a social justice perspective. The successful applicant must be able to manage a complex and multi-faceted workload and have demonstrated experience in national/regional level in youth, education and or community engagement programmes. This position reports to the Head of Youth Programmes and is based at SAIIA’s head office in Johannesburg.
KEY RESPONSIBILITIES
Provide administrative and logistical support as well as oversight of all programme activities, events, meetings and conferences hosted by Youth@SAIIA;
Run workshops, national school activities, conferences and Youth@SAIIA general events with support from fellow staff and youth network;
Support the Head of Youth with conceptual and proposal development to ensure financial sustainability of the programme;
Manage donor reports and project budgets for the successful implementation of our programmes;
Represent Youth@SAIIA at stakeholder consultations and partner meetings;
Write topics packs and resource materials for participants and educators;
Support our growing policy and youth participation work (especially in areas related to climate change; gender; health; and more generally sustainable development); and
Manage a team of staff including a youth cohort/network.
EXPERIENCE, SKILLS, QUALIFICATIONS
A relevant bachelor’s degree and postgraduate qualification (social sciences, education, environment) or any equivalent combination of education and experience;
A minimum of 8-10 years of experience with small to large-scale project/programme management in a developing country context;
A minimum of 3 years of experience in climate policy, environmental/sustainable development education and youth advocacy;
Experience with grant and proposal writing for national and international donors;
Excellent demonstrated written, presentation, communication and organisational skills;
Good planning and organisational skills;
Experience with financial management tools and systems;
Good management and interpersonal skills and ability to manage and work with a diverse team;
Experience with school negotiation and debate programmes advantageous;
Passion for working with high school learners, teachers, and youth participants; and
Driver’s Licence and own vehicle

