About the job:
The CSIR has a vacancy for
Testing Officer in
the Mining and Training and Testing impact area within the Mining cluster. The incumbent will be responsible to provide quotations for, arrange and carry out mechanical testing and related activities as part of the day-to-day operations of the mechanical test laboratory, and to ensure compliance with safety and quality requirements and achieving operational targets. The position is based in Cottesloe.
Key responsibilities:
Ensure that testing of standardized (routine) tests are carried out according to established test methods (national, international and in-house developed) and standard operating procedures in order to comply with laboratory quality standards:;
Provide guidance to laboratory assistants in sample preparation and test set-up to ensure validity of test results and to prevent damage to equipment;
Compile test certificates in line with laboratory quality standards to deliver a professional product to the customer. This includes the technical content of the document but also correct spelling and grammar, style and layout;
Accountable for ensuring traceability of test items throughout their life in the laborator;
Be responsible for all technical aspects related to the use of the various test machines in order to carry out tests safely and correctly;
Ensure machine and related equipment are serviceable and safe to use;
Customer liaison from initial test enquiry to final delivery of the test certificate is the responsibility of the test officer;
Handle customer applications and requests for testing to ensure that customer needs are understood and the customer is informed of the services the laboratory can offer;
Liaise with customers regarding scheduling of tests, special requirements for testing and to keep customers updated with progress or to notify customer of special circumstances that hinder completion of the work;
Be accountable for the safety of persons involved in the execution of a test and that of visitors witnessing the test;
Compile quotations in line with the laboratory pricing model in order to meet the financial targets of the laboratory;
Qualifications, skills and experience:
A National Diploma in Mechanical Engineering or equivalent relevant NQF level 6 qualification;
A minimum of three years’ experience in operating machines, using measuring equipment, dealing with customers, writing technical reports, and processing data is required for the candidate to be able to operate at the required level of proficiency that this position demands;
Experience working with hazardous substances;
Computer Literacy (Microsoft Office Package) skills;
Verbal and written communication skills;
Knowledge of ISO 17025;
Technical proficiency / aptitude;
Ability to pay attention to detail;
All international qualifications require an evaluation report / certificate issued by the South African Qualifications Authority (SAQA).

Requirements:
Senior Certificate or accredited equivalent, and relevant administrative qualification (graduates encouraged to apply)
At least 5 years’ experience with UCT administration and finance systems (SAP or any other ERP system), preferably in a research environment OR significant comparable administrative experience.
Excellent proficiency in English written/verbal communication
Excellent organisational and time management skills
Proven project management, networking and outreach skills
Ability to interact appropriately with a very diverse range of people
Ability to work in a diverse team and under pressure
Ability to work flexibly, independently and accurately with minimal supervision
Willingness to observe normal office hours on campus
Extensive knowledge of MS Word, MS Powerpoint, MS Excel
Advantageous:
Basic understanding of Drupal or similar software would be an advantage
Previous experience using Dropbox would be an advantage
Knowledge of the NRF funding system would be an advantage
Ability to retrieve law sources using online databases would be an advantage
Responsibilities:
Responsibilities include but are not limited to:
FINANCIAL AND PROJECT ADMINISTRATION:
Regular fortnightly and ad hoc funds reporting;
Open, close and manage cost fund centres;
Responsibility for financial processing, journal transfers, income receipting and other regular financial transactions, including P-Card administration;
Assist in monitoring and plan budgets, income and expenditure;
Assist in monitoring payroll and related processes; including payments to contractors;
Identify, investigate and reconcile anomalies in expenditure and income;
Administer petty cash;
Co-ordinate efficient day-to-day running of MLiA;
Responsibility for MLiA course administration and student records;
Manage physical infrastructure (space) and assets.
RESEARCH ADMINISTRATION AND SUPPORT
Monitor funding alerts and co-ordinate submission of funding applications;
Assist with preparation of annual financial and research reports to donors, funders, faculty and university;
Ensuring publications are up to date;
Manage online funding and rating applications, scholarship nominations etc;
Support researchers by facilitating travel and research dissemination;
Ensuring publication recording.
RESEARCH CAPACITY BUILDING SUPPORT and MARKETING
Logistic arrangements for workshops, conferences, short courses and training events (including refreshments and catering arrangements);
Ensure timeous and responsive communication with all interested parties, which strengthens the networks of collaborators and researchers;
Support and monitor website content and maintenance.
COMMUNICATION / CO-ORDINATION
Co-ordinate meetings, schedules and diaries;
Engage in regular networking communication with research partners and collaborators;
